What was rewritten: this cell starts the same pinned buyer runtime the original
registered. The original is an Agent Plugins v1 package with no code of its
own: a manifest that adds one MCP server (`npx -y @harrylabsj/kiwi@0.12.24 mcp
serve`) to every session, and a sourcing skill beside it. Here the cell speaks
MCP's stdio transport to that same runtime itself -- an initialize handshake,
then one tools/call or tools/list per child process -- so it needs no MCP
server list, no config edit and no other cell. The 13 tools the runtime
exposes are registered for the model under their own names with the arguments
their descriptions name, and a 14th, kiwi_tools, asks the installed runtime
what it answers; /kiwi does the same from the operator's side. The config
keys carry over as the section: the launcher, the pinned package, a timeout
and the runtime's store path. The skill's rules -- the search, RFQ, negotiate,
agreement, handoff loop, the approval gates on accept and handoff, the
dual-source search with its labels, the price kinds, and what a CommerceIntent
may carry -- are the cell's primer, so (help :kiwi) teaches what SKILL.md
taught. Nothing is fetched but the pinned npm package, and the runtime's own
catalog and merchant traffic is the same the plugin made.

What was left out: the prose skill as a separate file (its rules live in the
primer and in the tool descriptions), the Chinese translation of that skill,
and the long-lived MCP session (each call is its own child process, which the
runtime's SQLite store makes free). The plugin's README notes an optional
Ed25519 signing key directory for identity-gated reads; here it is read from
the environment the launcher inherits, not from the section.
